# ==============================================================================
# .env.playwright.local — SCOPED browser-login secrets (GITIGNORED, never commit)
# ==============================================================================
#
# DO NOT COPY THIS FILE BY HAND. Generate the real one:
#
#   npm run secrets:sync      # writes .env.playwright.local for the current TEST_ENV
#   npm run secrets:check     # exit 1 when it is stale (pre-flight also BLOCKs on that)
#
# The names are derived from what the suites, test-data and scripts/lib/user-roles.mjs
# declare; the values come from .env.local (the source the seeders set passwords from).
# A hand-kept copy drifted: REG-2026-10-14-1556 lost 120 of 124 cases of suites
# 089/091 to one name (TEST_USER_PASSWORD) that was in .env.local but not here.
# A new password goes into .env.local (team secret store), then re-run the sync.
#
# Wire it into .mcp.json, on EVERY playwright-* server:
#
#   "playwright-chrome": { "args": [ ..., "--config", "config/mcp-playwright-chrome.config.json",
#                                    "--secrets", ".env.playwright.local" ] }
#   (same for playwright-firefox / playwright-edge — see .mcp.json.example)
#
# The MCP reads the file at START: after a sync, reconnect playwright-* in /mcp —
# never while browser suites are running.
#
# Several envs at once (sessions on different stands sharing the lanes):
#   npm run secrets:sync:all  # writes .env.playwright.all — every env's names as NAME_<ENV>
# and point --secrets at .env.playwright.all instead; a session then types e.g.
# ORG_USER_PASSWORD_VCST_QA. A password that differs per env goes into .env.local as
# NAME_<ENV>=..., or into a gitignored .env.playwright.<env> with bare names.
#
# How --secrets works: when an agent calls `browser_type(text="B2B_USER_PASSWORD")`,
# the MCP substitutes the value and REDACTS it in logs — the plaintext never enters the
# agent's context or the transcript. Substitution triggers ONLY when the typed text
# EXACTLY equals a NAME in the file; a miss types the literal name.
#
# WHY A SEPARATE FILE (least privilege — do NOT point --secrets at .env.local):
#   .env.local also holds API tokens / keys. If those were in the --secrets file, a
#   malicious page or a prompt-injection could get the agent to type a PAT/API key into
#   an arbitrary web form. The sync writes LOGIN passwords only and never a token- or
#   key-shaped name, so the worst case is a low-value non-prod test password.
#
# Full rules: .claude/knowledge/execution/browser-lanes.md
# ==============================================================================
